parency, and proposed legislation; moderate for underlying spyware use.","contrary_evidence":["No general shutdowns or major encryption restrictions were reported; online political mobilization remained active."]},{"evidence_id":"E034","claim":"AMLO repeatedly criticized the Supreme Court, INE, TEPJF, and autonomous oversight institutions; BTI reported weakened or politically captured institutions and close MORENA ties among several new INE commissioners.","publisher":"Bertelsmann Stiftung","publication_date":"2024","source_confidence":"medium-high","period_fit":"broader AMLO-period condition continuing into 2023","ruler_attribution":"Strong for presidential rhetoric and political pressure; moderate for institutional-capture conclusions.","contrary_evidence":["The Supreme Court invalidated Plan B and electoral institutions continued operating."]},{"evidence_id":"E035","claim":"The Supreme Court invalidated the electoral Plan B package because Congress failed to comply with required publication and deliberation procedures, restoring the prior electoral rules.","publisher":"Suprema Corte de Justicia de la Nación","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"direct target-year evidence","ruler_attribution":"Strong for the attempted reform; strong evidence of judicial constraint, not voluntary presidential restraint.","contrary_evidence":["The ruling was primarily procedural and did not resolve every substantive electoral-design issue."]},{"evidence_id":"E036","claim":"AMLO’s Plan B electoral reform, approved by Congress in February 2023, would substantially reduce the INE’s budget, personnel, and office space, impairing impartial electoral oversight.","publisher":"Bertelsmann Stiftung","publication_date":"2024","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"direct 2023 legislative evidence with 2022 origins","ruler_attribution":"Strong administration and governing-coalition attribution.","contrary_evidence":["Government presented the reform as democratic modernization and the broader constitutional reform lacked the required supermajority."]},{"evidence_id":"E037","claim":"Mexico retained competitive multiparty elections, routine alternation in power, a six-year nonrenewable presidential term, and realistic opposition opportunities, although criminal violence and institutional weaknesses constrained political freedom.","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"ruler-period baseline continuing into 2023","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate personal attribution; institutions predated AMLO.","contrary_evidence":["Criminal influence, campaign-finance violations, and pressure on election bodies limited the quality of competition."]},{"evidence_id":"E066","claim":"Journalists in Mexico faced continuing killings, disappearances, intimidation, and high impunity, particularly when reporting on crime and political-criminal links.","publisher":"bti-project.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E067","claim":"The Supreme Court invalidated the electoral Plan B reforms after finding that Congress had not followed required publication and deliberation procedures.","publisher":"www.internet2.scjn.gob.mx","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E070","claim":"The AMLO administration expanded the armed forces’ roles in internal security, customs, ports, infrastructure, and National Guard administration.","publisher":"bti-project.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E071","claim":"MORENA and its allies retained an ordinary legislative majority after 2021 but lacked a constitutional supermajority, while criminal groups constrained effective state authority in some regions.","publisher":"bti-project.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]}]}
{"iso3":"PAK","ruler_name":"Shehbaz Sharif","coverage":[{"methodology_id":"4B.1","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E033","E034","E035","E067","E071","E072"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.2","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E031","E032","E033","E034","E035"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.3","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E002","E003","E004","E029","E030","E032","E033"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.4","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E002","E003","E004","E028","E031","E032","E034","E035"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.5","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E035"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.6","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E029","E030","E031"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.7","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E027"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.8","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E028","E033","E034"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.9","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E002","E004","E029","E031"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.10","status":"research_blocked","evidence_ids":[],"reason":"Formatter supplied no explicit researcher coverage disposition."}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E002","claim":"Amnesty International warned that Pakistan’s announced plan to prosecute civilians under the Army Act and Official Secrets Act after the May 2023 unrest violated fair-trial protections; later Supreme Court litigation confirmed that the policy was constitutionally contested.","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-05-18","source_confidence":"medium_high","period_fit":"target_period_2023","ruler_attribution":"moderate; the federal government supported the legal framework, but individual prosecutorial and military decisions are not attributed to Sharif personally.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities argued that attacks on military installations required prosecution under applicable security laws."]},{"evidence_id":"E003","claim":"The U.S. State Department reported credible allegations of enforced disappearance, including claims that security forces in Balochistan disappeared activists, politicians, teachers, and other civilians.","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2024-04","source_confidence":"medium_high","period_fit":"target_period_2023","ruler_attribution":"low_to_moderate; direct prime-ministerial command is not established and security authority was shared with military and provincial institutions.","contrary_evidence":["Security authorities may attribute operations to counterterrorism and insurgency; independent access to Balochistan is restricted."]},{"evidence_id":"E004","claim":"Pakistan’s Supreme Court declared the military trials of civilians arrested in connection with the May 9 violence unlawful and directed that the accused be tried in ordinary criminal courts.","publisher":"Associated Press of Pakistan","publication_date":"2023-10-23","source_confidence":"medium_high","period_fit":"target_period_2023","ruler_attribution":"moderate; federal and caretaker authorities supported military trials, but direct Sharif instruction is not established.","contrary_evidence":["The Supreme Court suspended the effect of the ruling in December and permitted the trials to resume pending further proceedings."]},{"evidence_id":"E027","claim":"Women, Ahmadis, non-Muslim minorities, and marginalized ethnic groups had formal political rights but faced practical restrictions, unequal access, discriminatory voter arrangements, or military pressure.","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"medium","period_fit":"inherited conditions continuing during 2023","ruler_attribution":"Low to moderate; structural and provincial factors were significant, while federal electoral and security policies remained relevant.","contrary_evidence":["Joint electorate arrangements and reserved seats provided formal representation for non-Muslim minorities."]},{"evidence_id":"E028","claim":"Courts sometimes allowed peaceful marches blocked by administrators, found caretaker overreach, and declared civilian military trials unconstitutional, although a later Supreme Court bench suspended that ruling.","publisher":"Human Rights Commission of Pakistan","publication_date":"2024","source_confidence":"medium-high","period_fit":"target year 2023","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; Sharif’s government did not fully control the courts but its policies were repeatedly challenged.","contrary_evidence":["Judicial interventions show that meaningful institutional checks survived."]},{"evidence_id":"E029","claim":"Pakistan imposed internet and platform restrictions, including a four-day shutdown in May 2023 to impede support for Imran Khan; journalist Imran Riaz Khan disappeared during the crackdown and returned in September.","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"medium-high","period_fit":"target year 2023","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to strong for federal telecommunications and security policy.","contrary_evidence":["The government could justify shutdowns as temporary public-order measures during violent unrest."]},{"evidence_id":"E030","claim":"Pakistan’s political and military elites retained broad control over media; regulators favored government interests, journalists faced administrative and criminal sanctions, and coverage of military interference in politics was highly constrained.","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2023–2026","source_confidence":"medium","period_fit":"inherited condition continuing during 2023","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; Sharif’s government influenced regulators and used legal tools, while military control was longstanding and partly autonomous.","contrary_evidence":["Pakistan retained a diverse private media sector and some independent reporting."]},{"evidence_id":"E031","claim":"Sharif’s government rushed through PEMRA and Official Secrets amendments shortly before Parliament’s dissolution, without adequate civil-society consultation, creating risks of censorship and journalist harassment.","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2023-08","source_confidence":"medium-high","period_fit":"target year 2023","ruler_attribution":"Strong for Sharif’s government and Parliament; implementation depended on regulators and security agencies.","contrary_evidence":["The government presented the measures as journalist-protection, disinformation, cybercrime, and espionage reforms."]},{"evidence_id":"E032","claim":"After the 9 May unrest, authorities used military custody, military courts, enforced disappearances, intimidation, and apparent political pressure to weaken PTI; some leaders were released after leaving the party or publicly condemning the unrest.","publisher":"Human Rights Commission of Pakistan","publication_date":"2024","source_confidence":"medium-high","period_fit":"target year 2023","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to strong for the federal coalition and security partners; direct personal direction by Sharif is not established.","contrary_evidence":["The state had a legitimate interest in prosecuting attacks on military and public sites."]},{"evidence_id":"E033","claim":"Following the 9 May unrest, police detained more than 4,000 people, including opposition members and some people not shown to have engaged in violence.","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-05-20","source_confidence":"medium-high","period_fit":"target year 2023","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to strong; federal coalition and security agencies jointly shaped the response.","contrary_evidence":["PTI supporters committed serious violence against military installations, police, ambulances, schools, and public property."]},{"evidence_id":"E034","claim":"Provincial elections were repeatedly postponed and caretaker governments remained in office beyond ordinary limits; HRCP reported a Lahore High Court finding that Punjab’s caretaker government exceeded its mandate by making major policy decisions.","publisher":"Human Rights Commission of Pakistan","publication_date":"2024","source_confidence":"medium-high","period_fit":"target year 2023","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; responsibility was shared with PTI provincial governments, ECP, courts, federal coalition, and caretaker administrations.","contrary_evidence":["The federal government cited administrative, security, and funding constraints, and the provincial assemblies had been dissolved by PTI-led governments."]},{"evidence_id":"E035","claim":"Pakistan retained formal civilian institutions, but the military remained more powerful than the elected government and judiciary in setting the boundaries of national security and civilian politics.","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"medium","period_fit":"inherited condition applicable during 2023","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; Sharif inherited the imbalance and generally accommodated the military rather than reducing its political role.","contrary_evidence":["Parliament, courts, parties, and civil society remained active and sometimes constrained the executive."]},{"evidence_id":"E067","claim":"Provincial elections were postponed and caretaker governments remained controversial, with HRCP reporting that the Lahore High Court found the Punjab caretaker government had exceeded its mandate.","publisher":"hrcp-web.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E071","claim":"The Pakistani government imposed internet and social-media restrictions, including a four-day shutdown in May 2023, amid the confrontation involving Imran Khan and the military establishment.","publisher":"freedomhouse.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E072","claim":"After the 9 May violence, the state conducted mass arrests and used military-court and security measures against PTI supporters, with HRCP reporting intimidation, disappearances, and political engineering.","publisher":"hrcp-web.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]}]}
