{"iso3":"THA","ruler_name":"Prayut Chanocha","coverage":[{"methodology_id":"4B.1","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E024","E029","E031","E032","E056","E061","E062","E063","E067"],"reason":"Election and institutional-order evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.2","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E029","E031","E067"],"reason":"Military-entrenched constitutional-order evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.3","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E025","E026","E027","E028","E030","E067"],"reason":"Speech, prosecution, and media evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.4","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E024","E029","E031","E032","E067"],"reason":"Election and institutional evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.5","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E024","E026","E030","E067"],"reason":"Digital and media-control evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.6","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E025","E026","E027","E030","E067"],"reason":"Information-environment evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.7","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E027","E067"],"reason":"Speech-law evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.8","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E024","E028","E029","E031","E067"],"reason":"Senate, courts, and detention evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.9","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E030","E067"],"reason":"Digital-freedom evidence retained."},{"methodology_id":"4B.10","status":"covered","evidence_ids":["E032","E067"],"reason":"Election-observer and democratic-posture evidence retained."}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E024","claim":"The Election Commission referred a case against Move Forward leader Pita Limjaroenrat to the Constitutional Court, which suspended him from parliamentary duties while reviewing an alleged media-shareholding violation.","publisher":"The Nation Thailand","publication_date":"2023-07-19","source_confidence":"medium","period_fit":"target_period","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; action was taken by the EC and Constitutional Court rather than directly by Prayut, though both operated within the inherited institutional order.","contrary_evidence":["The case followed formal constitutional procedures and was subject to judicial review."]},{"evidence_id":"E025","claim":"Outside election periods, Thailand’s mainstream media tended to be constrained in critical reporting; defamation and cybercrime laws were used to harass journalists, and self-censorship concerning the monarchy was widespread.","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"target_period_and_inherited_environment","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; legal and regulatory pressures were maintained by the government, while media ownership effects were not exclusively state-controlled.","contrary_evidence":["Election-period coverage allowed substantial political debate and independent online sources remained active."]},{"evidence_id":"E026","claim":"Thailand’s 2023 election produced a civilian-led government but media ownership remained concentrated among oligarchs linked to royal, military, and business interests; media independence and political reporting were constrained.","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"target_period_and_inherited_structure","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; government laws and regulators affected media conditions, while private and elite ownership networks were broader than Prayut personally.","contrary_evidence":["Opposition and pro-reform media existed and electoral debate was more open than under direct junta rule."]},{"evidence_id":"E027","claim":"Thailand’s lèse-majesté, sedition, and computer-crime frameworks constrained political criticism and contributed to arbitrary arrest, intimidation, and self-censorship.","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"inherited_and_target_period","ruler_attribution":"Strong for continued enforcement and legal environment; individual prosecutions involved police, prosecutors, and courts.","contrary_evidence":["The law was publicly debated and Move Forward campaigned on reforming it."]},{"evidence_id":"E028","claim":"Authorities continued prosecuting activists and critics in 2023; HRW recorded at least 258 people prosecuted in relation to democracy protests or online comments under lèse-majesté charges, while courts imposed restrictive bail and pretrial detention.","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2024","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"target_period","ruler_attribution":"Strong for the national legal and prosecutorial environment under Prayut until August; weaker for continuation under the successor government.","contrary_evidence":["Some defendants obtained bail and opposition parties campaigned successfully."]},{"evidence_id":"E029","claim":"Thailand’s 2017 constitution and appointed Senate preserved military influence over prime-ministerial selection and limited the effective power of elected representatives; courts and independent bodies were politically contested.","publisher":"Bertelsmann Stiftung","publication_date":"2024","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"inherited_and_target_context","ruler_attribution":"Strong for the institutional inheritance from Prayut’s junta and government; weaker for events after January 2023.","contrary_evidence":["Competitive parties, civil society, and the 2023 opposition victory provided real democratic openings."]},{"evidence_id":"E030","claim":"Thailand’s internet environment was Not Free in 2023; ahead of the election, state-sponsored disinformation targeted opposition actors, activists faced arrest and surveillance, authorities used the Computer-Related Crimes Act, and digital regulators lacked strong independence.","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"target_period","ruler_attribution":"Strong to moderate; government digital institutions and regulatory structures were maintained under Prayut, while individual campaigns require separate proof.","contrary_evidence":["No nationwide internet shutdown was reported and social media remained a space for critical speech."]},{"evidence_id":"E031","claim":"Move Forward won the 2023 election but was blocked from forming a government by the military-appointed Senate; Pheu Thai subsequently formed a coalition including former military-aligned parties.","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2024","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"target_period","ruler_attribution":"Strong for the inherited constitutional structure; moderate for individual Senate actions and post-election coalition bargaining.","contrary_evidence":["Prayut left office through a constitutional transition rather than a new coup."]},{"evidence_id":"E032","claim":"ANFREL’s final observation described the 2023 election as showing accountability, transparency, and inclusiveness, while also issuing recommendations to improve electoral democracy; advance voting and Election Day were peaceful and orderly.","publisher":"Asian Network for Free Elections","publication_date":"2023-08-15","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"target_period","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; election administration was formally independent, while the governing constitutional environment was inherited from Prayut’s military-backed order.","contrary_evidence":["Senate participation in executive formation and unequal institutional conditions limited the consequences of the election."]},{"evidence_id":"E056","claim":"Information was obtainable from independent and international sources, but criminal defamation, lèse-majesté, sedition, computer-crime laws, surveillance, and intimidation encouraged self-censorship and limited transparency.","publisher":"bti-project.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E061","claim":"Thai authorities continued prosecuting protesters and online critics under lèse-majesté and related laws, with Human Rights Watch recording at least 258 prosecutions in 2023.","publisher":"www.hrw.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E062","claim":"Move Forward won the most House seats in 2023, but the military-appointed Senate helped prevent it from forming a government; Pheu Thai then formed a coalition and Srettha became prime minister.","publisher":"freedomhouse.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E063","claim":"Prayut formally headed the cabinet but governed within a system where the monarchy, military, junta-appointed Senate, courts, and coalition institutions constrained effective authority.","publisher":"bti-project.org","publication_date":"unknown_not_exposed_by_source","source_confidence":"unknown_not_recorded","period_fit":"unknown_not_recorded","ruler_attribution":"unknown_not_recorded","contrary_evidence":[]},{"evidence_id":"E067","claim":"Thailand ended 2023 with genuine electoral competition but without a normal democratic transfer from the military-backed order; the appointed Senate, contested courts, restrictive speech laws, media pressures, and digital controls limited the practical effect of the opposition victory.","publisher":"Cross-source synthesis","publication_date":"2023-2024","source_confidence":"medium","period_fit":"target_period","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to strong for the inherited constitutional and legal order maintained under Prayut.","contrary_evidence":["Prayut left office through a constitutional transition and the election produced a major opposition victory."]}]}
{"iso3":"TUR","ruler_name":"Recep Tayyip Erdoğan","coverage":[{"methodology_id":"4B.1","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E045","E046","E047","E048","E076","E079","E080"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.2","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E040","E045","E046","E047","E048"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.3","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E039","E043","E044","E046"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.4","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E039","E040","E042"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.5","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E039","E040","E044","E045"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.6","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E043","E044"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.7","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E041","E046"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.8","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E047","E048"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.9","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E043"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."},{"methodology_id":"4B.10","status":"partially_covered","evidence_ids":["E040","E041","E042","E048"],"reason":"Contains restored reviewed evidence."}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E039","claim":"Civil-society organizations and human-rights defenders reported persistent pressure and targeting, while numerous ECtHR rulings finding violations of fundamental freedoms remained unaddressed.","publisher":"OSCE/ODIHR","publication_date":"2023-09-29","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"direct 2023 evidence with inherited structural conditions","ruler_attribution":"Strong national institutional attribution; individual legal actions require case-specific evidence.","contrary_evidence":["Civil society continued to organize, and some courts issued rights-protective decisions."]},{"evidence_id":"E040","claim":"The presidential system centralized policymaking and regulatory authority, weakened separation of powers, left the executive mainly accountable through elections, and was accompanied by pressure on opposition mayors, politicized administration, and executive influence over judges and prosecutors.","publisher":"European Commission","publication_date":"2023-11-08","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"structural condition operative during 2023","ruler_attribution":"Strong institutional attribution to Erdoğan’s presidency and governing coalition; some arrangements predated 2023.","contrary_evidence":["Parliament, courts, municipalities, and elections retained formal powers."]},{"evidence_id":"E041","claim":"Women were underrepresented in political leadership, no woman ran for president, and campaign rhetoric included discriminatory messages about LGBTI people, migrants, and refugees.","publisher":"OSCE/ODIHR","publication_date":"2023-09-29","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"direct target-year evidence","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; party structures and social conditions also contributed, while national law and executive rhetoric shaped the environment.","contrary_evidence":["Women’s representation in parliament increased from 17 to 20 percent."]},{"evidence_id":"E042","claim":"Election administration was technically efficient but lacked transparency; the Supreme Electoral Council published few decisions, complaint procedures were insufficiently reasoned, and effective legal remedy for electoral disputes was not ensured.","publisher":"OSCE/ODIHR","publication_date":"2023-09-29","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"direct target-year evidence","ruler_attribution":"Moderate; the institutional framework was established by law, while the election body retained operational autonomy.","contrary_evidence":["The election administration met legal deadlines and voting was generally orderly."]},{"evidence_id":"E043","claim":"During the 2023 election period, the Disinformation Law was used against opposition members and critical journalists; websites and social-media content were blocked or removed, and online speech faced criminal penalties.","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","source_confidence":"medium-high","period_fit":"law inherited from 2022; implementation directly relevant to 2023","ruler_attribution":"Strong government-policy attribution; enforcement was conducted by courts, regulators, police, and communications agencies.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities presented the law as a measure against deliberate false information and threats to public order."]},{"evidence_id":"E044","claim":"Public broadcasters clearly favored ruling parties and candidates, while legal restrictions, arrests, prosecutions, website blocking, and online-content removal contributed to self-censorship and reduced informed electoral choice.","publisher":"OSCE/ODIHR","publication_date":"2023-09-29","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"direct target-year evidence","ruler_attribution":"Strong political-environment attribution; media regulation and ownership also involved state bodies and private businesses.","contrary_evidence":["Some private media supplied more diverse coverage and opposition outlets remained active."]},{"evidence_id":"E045","claim":"Erdoğan campaigned during official duties, inaugurated infrastructure, and announced social benefits during the campaign; campaign-finance rules and enforcement were insufficiently transparent.","publisher":"OSCE/ODIHR","publication_date":"2023-09-29","source_confidence":"high","period_fit":"direct target-year evidence","ruler_attribution":"Strong for Erdoğan’s use of official functions; broader campaign-finance regulation involved parliament and election authorities.","contrary_evidence":["Opposition parties campaigned